WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.160
Marine ecology in the early 1960s was still mostly descriptive: who lived where, and

2
00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:14.600
who ate whom. Then Robert Paine kept one species of sea star off a patch of rocky

3
00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:18.840
shore. At Mukkaw Bay, Washington, he began doing just that.

4
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:24.580
By his 1966 report, the patch held 8 species on the rock instead of 15.

5
00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:29.440
Taking a predator away had left fewer kinds of species holding the rock.

6
00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:33.040
How can a species that eats others keep more of them around?

7
00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:36.260
The sea star was Pisaster ochraceus.

8
00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:40.200
It ate mussels, barnacles, chitons, limpets and snails.

9
00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:44.800
Paine wanted to know what would happen if one member of the community were taken

10
00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:48.040
out. He kept Pisaster out of one patch of shore.

11
00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:51.480
He left an adjacent stretch alone as a control.

12
00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:54.640
Then he surveyed both along line transects.

13
00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:58.560
The test was simple: remove a species, then measure the community.

14
00:00:59.733 --> 00:01:01.053
Why would the shore change?

15
00:01:01.953 --> 00:01:04.213
The mechanism was competition for space.

16
00:01:05.113 --> 00:01:07.613
On a rocky shore, space is a limiting resource.

17
00:01:08.533 --> 00:01:10.473
It is as limiting as light in a forest.

18
00:01:11.493 --> 00:01:13.413
Mussels are strong competitors for rock.

19
00:01:14.253 --> 00:01:17.933
With Pisaster eating them, space kept opening for weaker competitors.

20
00:01:20.117 --> 00:01:21.717
Now take the sea star away.

21
00:01:22.717 --> 00:01:24.977
Without Pisaster, the mussels spread over the rock.

22
00:01:25.837 --> 00:01:27.417
They won the competition for space.

23
00:01:28.317 --> 00:01:30.297
The weaker competitors lost their footing.

24
00:01:31.137 --> 00:01:33.137
The patch lost species from the rock surface.

25
00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:35.280
Count the species.

26
00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:40.880
In the removal patch, the number of species holding space on the rock fell from 15

27
00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:44.640
to 8. The control did not lose species in the same way.

28
00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:49.260
So the sea star did not help the shore by eating species that harmed the others.

29
00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:53.220
It raised the richness of species on the rock by freeing space.

30
00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:59.520
The 1966 paper was a strong local experiment, with one removal patch and one

31
00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:00.520
control.

32
00:02:00.933 --> 00:02:03.153
Ecologists call this keystone predation.

33
00:02:04.013 --> 00:02:08.513
A consumer that prefers to eat a strong competitor indirectly benefits the weaker

34
00:02:08.513 --> 00:02:14.253
ones. A keystone species has an effect on its community that is large relative to

35
00:02:14.253 --> 00:02:18.653
its abundance. The most common species is not a keystone by default.

36
00:02:19.553 --> 00:02:23.013
Keep the rule: judge a species by its effect, not by its numbers.
